    title: "Russian strikes hit Kyiv after Witkoff meets Putin",
    summary: "Ukraine says 'appeasement' will not end war as Russian attacks kill 1, injure 22 in its capital.",
    preview: "Ukraine says ‘appeasement’ will not end war as Russian attacks kill 1, injure 22 in its capital. Russia and Ukraine have traded a new barrage of attacks after a United States envoy met Russia’s president in St Petersburg to push for renewed talks on ending the war. Russian attacks on Kyiv overnight into Thursday killed at least one person and injured 22, including four children, Ukraine’s national emergency service said.",
    body: "Ukraine says ‘appeasement’ will not end war as Russian attacks kill 1, injure 22 in its capital.\n\nRussia and Ukraine have traded a new barrage of attacks after a United States envoy met Russia’s president in St Petersburg to push for renewed talks on ending the war.\n\nRussian attacks on Kyiv overnight into Thursday killed at least one person and injured 22, including four children, Ukraine’s national emergency service said.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3Russia releases American Stephen Hubbard after four years in prison\n\n- list 2 of 3Russia to send first diesel tanker to the US amid Trump deal\n\n- list 3 of 3Russia willing to resume peace talks with Ukraine, US after Witkoff visit\n\nMetro infrastructure was also damaged at the Pivdenny Bridge, which spans the Dnipro River, Kyiv Mayor Vitali Klitschko said.\n\nIn Russia, Moscow Mayor Sergei Sobyanin said two people were wounded after Ukraine launched a barrage of 630 drones on the region, 163 of which were sent to the capital city. He said most of the drones were intercepted.\n\nThe governor of the nearby Tula region said Russian air defences destroyed 70 drones. Another man was wounded in the Lipetsk region, its governor said.\n\nThe exchange of attacks came after US special envoy Steve Witkoff met with Russian President Vladimir Putin at Constantine Palace in St Petersburg on Wednesday.\n\nDuring Witkoff’s visit, Russia released jailed US teacher Stephen Hubbard as a goodwill gesture, prompting the US envoy to praise Putin as a “humanitarian”.\n\nUS President Donald Trump later said he believed the Russia-Ukraine war may be as close as it’s ever been to ending.\n\nBut the Kremlin was more guarded. While it said Moscow was “in principle” willing to restart US-mediated talks with Ukraine, it warned there was little “substance” in Ukrainian and European peace proposals.\n\nIn recent days, a frustrated Trump has once again pivoted away from Ukraine and back to Putin, trumpeting what he said was a deal for heavily sanctioned Russia to sell diesel and reduce fuel prices sent soaring by the US-Israel war on Iran.\n\nTrump also delayed a new wave of Russia-related sanctions, a decision he defended on Wednesday as necessary to secure Hubbard’s release.\n\nWitkoff told Putin Washington was “grateful” for the diesel deal, which sparked outrage in Kyiv and across Europe.\n\nThe war, Europe’s worst since World War II, has killed tens of thousands of people since Russia’s full-scale February 2022 invasion of its neighbour.\n\nPutin has consistently rejected face-to-face talks with Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy and insisted Moscow intends to seize the rest of eastern Ukraine.\n\nUkrainian Foreign Minister Andrii Sybiha on Thursday said “appeasement” would not stop Russia’s war campaign.\n\n“Appeasement, servility, and flattery will not stop this war,” he wrote in a post on X. “Only strength will.”",
